    {
      "dom_id": "question-68204",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14202290/assets/1a5313995e9740a61b455a9c368aca6223a80c8bbea659f06ec2a63c8260747b.png)\n\nKaren decides to walk from her workplace to her house. There is a proportional relationship between the distance $x$ (in kilometers) she covers and the time $y$ (in hours) it takes her to cover that distance.\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the graph above?",
      "worked_solution": "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14202290/assets/bec01f9dc01b340075f09dd459571fe8199116f18a6ab912eb958c7a6f9ee4f2.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
